That script is part of the build setup (CI runs it before publishing) because
this repository was authored in an environment with no access to gnu.org, and
reproducing legal text from memory is the one place where "good enough" is not
acceptable: a modified LGPL must be the FSF's exact text or it is not the LGPL.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
How this license is applied in this repository (the operative terms)
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1. The work covered by this file is everything under common/, fabric/,
   neoforge/, docs/, tools/, deltas/, gradle/ and the root files, except where a
   file's own header states otherwise.

2. "Combined Work" in the sense of LGPL-3.0 section 4 means Minecraft plus this
   mod. Linking this library with a proprietary game is permitted; distributing
   the combination is subject to section 4's conditions, which in practice means:
   ship the mod as a separate jar (as `remapJar` produces) and keep this license
   and NOTICE available next to it. Do not ship Minecraft's classes inside this
   jar; the build is configured so that it cannot.

3. Reverse-engineering permission (section 4's "for modifying the work" clause)
   is granted for the Minecraft classes this mod injects into, to the extent
   Minecraft's own EULA permits it. Mixin application at runtime is done by
   Fabric Loader / NeoForge, not by code in this jar.

4. Contributions: submitting a patch to this repository grants the same LGPL-3.0
   license to the contributed material, and by contributing you confirm you may
   grant it. Contribution rules, including the DCO-style sign-off, are in
   CONTRIBUTING.md.

5. Third-party notices, including the licenses of Mixin, MixinExtras, LWJGL and
   Fabric Loader, are in NOTICE. None of their sources are vendored here; they are
   resolved as Gradle dependencies.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Linking exception used by this project
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The LGPL-3.0 "Combined Work" mechanism is used rather than the (L)GPL-2.1-style
"linking exception" text, so no additional permission is granted beyond the
license itself. In particular, a modpack that merely ships aetherium-fabric.jar
or aetherium-neoforge.jar alongside other mods is not a derivative work of
Aetherium; nothing about those other mods' sources becomes owed to anyone.
